A||N||25||Chris McDonald||Project High MPG||||5||
Z||000000||Chris McDonald||01-11-2001||01:59 PM||||Last year I was transfered to a new office that resulted in a 50 plus mile round trip to and from work.  Since then, I picked up a 1994 two door GEO Metro with the 3 cylinder engine five speed trans and a/c.  So far, I average about 45 mpg in city driving. At the current and future price of gas I feel I made a wise investment.  However, a Metro is not an attractive or sporty looking vehicle.   <P>There is some significant technology employed on the Metro to achieve such MPG with pretty decent acceleration and performance. Considerable weight reduction and a 58 hsp engine seem to contribute to the fuel economy.<P>Here is the idea.  Instead of building the high hsp Fiero--what are your thoughts on the ultimate in MPG?  <P>We could start out with an engine swap.  My thought would be a Metro motor (however, we could consider anything).  We need to keep this project powered by gasoline or diesel, no electric cars.  The price has to stay low as well to justify the means.  Maybe you have some ideas how to stretch out the stock Fiero engine.<P>We would need to reduce weight.  Any ideas?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||lou_dias||01-11-2001||02:22 PM||||Well, the base coup with a 5 speed gets 39+mpg.  This is with no power options.<P>In 1988, a Canadian company developed and aluminum frame for the 89 Fiero  prototypes.  Pretty sure you can still buy an aluminum 2.5 block and head.<P>An Aluminum Engine cradle would be sweet to and could balance the F/R weight ratio right away.  I think with all this, the Fiero could easily weight under 2000lbs, probably 1700.  The metro weighs 1700-1900lbs, right?<P>When doing an engine rebuild, go with a heavy flywheel (better fuel economy, more torque, less hp) also, I think the rod length to piston bore ratio comes into play.  I think the Ideal ratio is 1.75:1...<BR>I think the 3.1 v6 has this ratio, correct me if I'm wrong...<P>Over inflate your tires...<BR>Anybody know any other tricks?||||reg||1
Z||000002||ferg||01-11-2001||02:26 PM||||Weight reduction would help. Trying to put a less powerfull engine in that car would result in less milage than a stock 4 cyl. I think that you would use all the engine power just to move the car, this would cause the engine to work harder thus using more fuel. <P>I have no ideas for the diesel aspect. <P>You would probably be better off sticking w/ the metro, or if you really want to use a fiero, just drive it in stock form. <P>Your drive isn't all that bad compared w/ my commute. I drive 45 miles ONE WAY, and my daily driver is a Dodge dakota R/T! I average 15 mpg w/ speeds between 60-70.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||01-11-2001||02:29 PM||||You could consider switching to an ECM that supports Highway Mode (lean AFR/spark advanced cruise cycling). There are a couple of ways to do it. It would allow you to run up around 16-17:1 AFR during cruise.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Chris McDonald||01-11-2001||02:31 PM||||Ouch---15 mpg!  That has to add up over a month.  <P>I agree with the power to weight ratio.  The GEO weighs around 1,700 lbs.  That is the only way the 58 hsp can push the car.<P>Saftey is always a concern, I have noticed that Fiero doors are very heavy.  Anybody out there ever pulled one off.  Strained your back didn't ya.  <P>gutting the door could save alot of weight.  I was also wondering about all the ultra high strength steel behind the bumpers.  The side and rear windows could be swapped for plexi.<P>Would lowering the car or adding skirts do any good?  How about skinny tires?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||ferg||01-11-2001||02:51 PM||||I pass everything but a gas station.<P>you could gut the interior and put in aluminum, no dash just a steering wheel. and a seat.<P>just some quick calculations- if you drive 250 miles per week and get 45 mpg then your fuel consumption is about 5.5 gal/week, assuming you could get 39 mpg on a stock fiero your fuel consumption is about 6.4 gal/week, difference of 1 gal/week. That's about $1.50/week more for gas. Soooo- you have to ask yourself, does this $1.50 savings justify the expense of trying to gain 6 mpg on a fiero????||||reg||1
Z||000006||Black88GT||01-11-2001||02:56 PM||||The only way a Fiero is weighing less then 2000 lbs. is with an aluminum frame and gutted interior.<P>I think a coupe could get 30+ city gas mileage if it is in top notch running shape, and the driver shifts early and often.<P>  <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||01-11-2001||03:26 PM||||It has been studied (by BMW, no less!) and follows naturally from thermodynamic principles that the way to get the best gas mileage from a car is to have a very heavy foot and shift very short. For instance, when accelerating, accelerate very hard but shift at or below 2000 rpm for economy. The stuff about driving like you have and egg under your foot for gas mileage is bunk.<P><BR>BTW, a Geo doesn't have to be all bad. Check this out: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005148.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005148.html</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 01-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Chris McDonald||01-11-2001||03:40 PM||||You make a wise point.  But, why stop at 39 mpg?  If you could push the car to 55 mpg there would be a decent decrease in cost.  <P>Considering the cost of a new Hybrid you could do a whole lot to a Fiero and still spend much less.  It seems to me though that the ceiling of a gas powered car is no more than a realistic 50 mpg under perfect conditions.  Otherwise, we would see more efficient cars under gas power instead of hybrid.  Of course, hybrids don't create as much pollution.<P>I have heard rumor of $2 to $3 a gallon gas prices in the very real future.  <P>BTW, we have a 95 3500 series Chevy truck with the 454 fuel injected engine.  I average 7.5 mpg without hauling anything.  In three days I spent over $70 in gas just moving some heavy things around town with it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||ferg||01-11-2001||03:45 PM||||&gt;&gt;&gt;BTW, we have a 95 3500 series Chevy truck with the 454 fuel injected engine. I average 7.5 mpg without hauling anything. In three days I spent over $70 in gas just moving some heavy things around town with it.&lt;&lt;&lt;<P>WELCOME TO MY LIFE!<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||01-11-2001||03:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ferg:<BR><B>Weight reduction would help. Trying to put a less powerfull engine in that car would result in less milage than a stock 4 cyl. I think that you would use all the engine power just to move the car, this would cause the engine to work harder thus using more fuel. <BR>&lt;...&gt;<BR>Your drive isn't all that bad compared w/ my commute. I drive 45 miles ONE WAY, and my daily driver is a Dodge dakota R/T! I average 15 mpg w/ speeds between 60-70.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have found that my coupe doesn't get that much better mileage than my Formula. Both get around 20 mpg, but I have to drive the coupe nearly wide open to keep up with Atlanta commuter traffic. (75-80 MPH)<P>I lust after a Dakota R/T, but I *****  about my gas mileage, now. Might have to reconsider. (Of course I *could* go ahead and replace my TCC solenoid.)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfixit58||01-11-2001||04:22 PM||||First of all, a four cylindar w/overdrive is a MUST. To gain more gear, try goint to taller profile tires in the rear. Keep the width of the tires, both in the front and in the rear to a minimum to reduce your rolling resistance. <P>Use synthetic wheel bearing bearing grease.<P>Lower the car to reduce wind buffeting under the car.<P>Vent the hood behind the radiator to improve air flow through the radiator and over the car.<P>Use the hottest thermistat you can find to improve engine effeciency.<P>Remove all un-necessary weight: padding, passenger seat carpet and anything else that bolts in (Including heavy amplifiers and speakers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> ).<P>Do not use a wing. It adds down force and increases drag.<P>Use a GT style nose.<P>Use only a hard top car to avoid the added weight of the sun roof. <P>No power ANYTHING to keep it light.<P>Keep the car clean and waxed.<P>Use Tom's fixed headlight buckets and get rid of the pop-up lights.<P>Use a K$N air filter.<P>Use 5w-30 Moble One oil.<P>Try advancing the timing a degree at a time to gain the best efeciency at your average RPM.<P>Any more guys? That's all I can think of right now.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000012||stsmithgt||01-11-2001||04:28 PM||||I only put premuim in my tank so paying over 2.00 a gallon is a common sight.  Especially over this last summer.  I'll keep what horse power I have and maintain my gas milage.  I think I'm sacrificing enough by NOT putting in a V8.  Oh I miss the HP of a V8.||||reg||6
Z||000013||Black88GT||01-11-2001||04:55 PM||||How can you accelerate hard and shift and 2000?  If I accelerate hard my car is well over 2000 in a split second.  You would have to super super fast to shift before 2000 if you accelerate hard.<P>I guess it would be possible in a Metro<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000014||88formula||01-11-2001||05:05 PM||||The best way to gain gas mileage is to reduce the fieros weight and that could be expensive and thus cost prohibitive. <P>I think you guys should not worry about your gas mileage and just enjoy life while you can. What are you going to do die with a bunch of money in your hands smiling? It's only money!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Philphine||01-11-2001||06:22 PM||||would it do any good to switch to a newer 4cly.? like out of a new sunfire. i would think some efficiency gains have been made since the '80's. i guess the cost of swapping would have to be factored in though.||||reg||1
Z||000016||pHoOl||01-11-2001||06:36 PM||||I had a thought of if i had a four banger fiero, trying to put in a saturn four banger.  The only reason i'd consider it is the fact that it is all aluminum.  I'd assume that you'd get a big weight reduction.  And saturns are pretty quick for four bangers.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: phool88fiero<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||hugh||01-11-2001||06:47 PM||||Only drive downhill and coast a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Chris McDonald||01-11-2001||08:46 PM||||Okay,  I think we have something here.  If we are to attempt the ultimate MPG for a Fiero a few things have to be done.<P>*Engine swap to a lighter yet powerful engine like the aluminum Saturn 4 cyl.<P>*Synthetic/light weight oils in all possible areas.<P>*G.T. style front nose, lower the suspension, and run tall rear wheels while keeping them skinny all the way around.<P>*Plexi/lexan glass<P>*Gutting extra items.<P>*Get the engine as efficient as possible through correct engine temp, adjustments etc.<P>*Substitute light weight materials where possible.<P>*Vent the hood.<P>*No wings or extra body drag--maybe only a driver side rear view mirror.<P>*Good paint/wax<P>I guess all this could be done for $4,000 or less depending on the cost of the donor motor and wiring harness/computer.  <P>I guess you would have to save a lot of gas over a couple of years to cover the initial investment.<P>If you did it right, you could have your hot rod car for the weekends while you squeeze every precious drop of fuel out of the gas mizer during the week.<P>Okay--somebody donate the Saturn and I will find a suitable Fiero.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||ur2biteme||01-11-2001||10:14 PM||||why hasnt someone mentioned a quad 4 tuned for this sort of thing?  not only would you have much more power, but you'd get a weight drop (similar to that of the saturn engine) and the gas mileage.  that's why i'm gonna go with it.  there's no way i'd be wanting to lose much off my current freeway mileage on my car.  it's just too darn good.  <P>btw, i guess i developed the before mentioned driving habit by pure accident (because that's pretty close to how i shift, but closer to 2500-2700) and get about 40-42 mpg on the freeway.  <P>i'd say shave some weight, go to a more powerful, but smaller 4 banger (saturn i think is a 2.2, quad 4 at 2.3, maybe even that turbo 2.0 that has been mentioned) stay with the stock tire recommended for the coupes (175 70R14's i think) less restrictive exhaust, but not ENTIRELY eliminating back pressure (flowmaster, not a glasspack) and make sure the engine can breathe in freely.  get to your speed quickly and stay there when driving.  <P>------------------<BR>Bob's '87 econo coupe<BR>170k awaiting a tranny:(<BR>http://members.fortunecity.com/ur2biteme||||reg||1
Z||000020||Jimmy||01-12-2001||01:44 AM||||  I thought the best gas milage Fieros were the 84 2m4 with the MY9 4-Speed tranny.  Its fourth gear ratio was .73, its axle ratio was 3.32, and combined gave you an overall 2.42 ratio.  This is the best ratio for gas milage, the 5 speed Isuzus have an overall of 2.48.  <P>Jim <BR>87 GT T-TOP V8<BR>87 GT<BR>    ||||reg||1
Z||000021||frontal lobe||01-12-2001||01:57 AM||||If you are going to all that trouble, just drop in an electric motor/diesel hybrid.  Shouldn't be too hard!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000022||Will||01-12-2001||10:52 AM||||I also tend to be in the 2500 range for my shifts. I routinely go into 4th at 25 mph or so (unless I'm in a fast mood).<P>The Saturn twincam engine is an updated 2.4 litre Quad-4. In fact, the HO Quad 4 head will bolt on to the twin cam shortblock--but that's not an economy platform. There was an article on that in a recent Popular Hot Rodding, I think. The twincam and the Quad4 are both iron block aluminum head engines.<P>I think that beating the efficiency of an Iron Duck would be hard. The most notable improvement would come from converting an iron duck from TBI to sequential port FI. The use of a modern GM ECM with the "economy cruise" function would also help.<P>Replacing the glass with plex or lexan for weight savings is just not worth the trouble.<P>And I'd also prefer to keep the right side view mirror.||||reg||
Z||000023||red88||01-13-2001||10:31 AM||||I have an iron duke and I have gotten 45mpg by removing ac, using sentetic oil,small tires,k&h filter, side scoup, no power windows no powerlocks they add a lot of weight and I have a auto tranny, free flow cat and muffler. It only get about 35mpg now, 2 years later but I have about 250,000 miles on the orignal 4banger it still run but is waiting for a brake job and engine <BR>(burning oil bad) <BR> also my wife's  cutless gets 35mpg with a <BR>3.1 v6 AOD tranny <BR>  <BR> I feel safer in a fiero than a geo I have seen people killed in them at 5mph I have a friend who flipped his fiero 5 times after he hit a colvert and opend the door and walked away with a sore back and bruses. <BR> I hit a 15point buck(yes a 15 point deer) at 65 with mine all it did was bounce off the headlights pop off the caps and give me a funny look and ran off  <P>oh one thing to add keep up on tuneup and oil changes<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000024||theogre||01-13-2001||11:31 AM||||You appear to have missed an option.... You could use a small diesle. There are a number of small 3 & 4 cyl diesle you could use. I saw an MG once that had a diesle out of a refer trailer shoved into it.<P>If you found one of the old diesle VW rabbits that whole powertrain could probalby be transplanted with minimum pain. Those were even available as a turbodiesle setup.<P>replacing glass is a good idea at first. problem is most plastics don't like to be used like Fiero side windows are. You would'nt want to replace the windshield. The wipers would chew up most plastics pluss it's a safety problem. Replacing the back glass would be easy and work well since the plastic likes being supported all the way around the edge. Just the rear glas will save a bunch of weight. Since it's also flat it's just a matter of cutting sheet lexan and sticking it place.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Will||01-13-2001||11:41 AM||||If you transplant the diesel from a recent TDI VW, you might be able to use the VW 02A transaxle without an adapter plate. If you can do that, there is a kit that adds a 6th gear on above the OEM 5th for very low highway cruise RPM. That particular kit is only $600 or so from <A HREF="http://www.stealthracing.co.uk" TARGET=_blank>www.stealthracing.co.uk</A> ||||reg||1
